# Why don't smart people buy it

The most dangerous thing about writing an investment article is to write only the logic that you like.

The counter-controduction of the United States Regiment must be written in earnest, because they are not weak.

# First, 2024, the profit may be higher

Net profits in 2024, $35.8 billion, appear to be valuation anchors.

But that may be a special year for competition that is relatively mild, with less pressure on subsidies and more fully released profits.

If competition is regularized in the future, normal profits may not return to $35.8 billion.

Using 2024 profits as static PE may underestimate the true profit margin after the business model has been diluted by competition.

# Second, Ali doesn't have to make money

It's hard to explain how much money you burn when you're alone looking hungry or shopping.

But Ali may see it as a strategic entry to the group: Gold, the posse, the payment of the treasure, the local life of the community, and the reconstruction of the high-frequency consumption scene.

Whether this synergy can be realized or not, however, price wars may last longer than investors expect as long as Ali believes it to be of strategic value.

# And third, the rider's cost may just rise

The social security of riders, the regulation of algorithms and the protection of labour rights increase the costs of platform performance.

That's good, and it's cost.

If the future costs of riders rise and the platform cannot be fully transferred to users and businesses, the long-term profitability of the United States Corps will be reduced.

# And fourth, winning the war doesn't necessarily make money

The worst is not the American dollar’s share lost, but its share held, but at the cost of the user’s multiplatform pricing habit, increased bargaining power of traders, regulation of low commission space and rigid increases in the cost of riders.

The United States could win the industry and lose profit elasticity.

